      "id": 1,
      "text": "Douglas was originally a post office named Onyx before being renamed in 1903, likely in honor of Douglas Frantz.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/douglas_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area of Douglas was initially settled in the 1890s after the Cherokee Outlet opened to non-Indian homesteaders.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/douglas_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Denver, Enid and Gulf Railroad arrived in 1902, helping Douglas grow into a bustling regional hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/douglas_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Douglas once featured a substantial brick school built in 1911 with four classrooms, two halls, and a library.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/douglas_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Christian Union Church of Douglas was established in 1896 and served the community into the late twentieth century.",
